1
00:00:00,160 --> 00:00:04,160
Module 6, Video 6.3, Read Where the Work Stands from the Work.

2
00:00:04,840 --> 00:00:09,620
Um, so when a vendor providing systems in your ministry offers a figure for

3
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:15,520
how complete the work is, uh, why is that percentage an ineffective measure for a status update?

4
00:00:15,940 --> 00:00:19,380
Well, simply put, a percentage cannot be checked by anyone in the room.

5
00:00:19,640 --> 00:00:22,640
Right. And it completely fails to show what work remains.

6
00:00:23,060 --> 00:00:26,240
I mean, nobody can say what the rest of the work actually is.

7
00:00:26,700 --> 00:00:27,580
Because it's just a number.

8
00:00:27,940 --> 00:00:33,100
Exactly. Progress must be read from fixed form documents that record exactly what they were made from.

9
00:00:33,220 --> 00:00:40,720
Mm-hmm. This allows anyone at any time to generate a report from those documents that gives the exact same verifiable answer.

10
00:00:41,220 --> 00:00:46,420
Okay, so how does the trace report mechanically follow the work to prevent your program from becoming,

11
00:00:47,120 --> 00:00:50,660
say, fragmented, siloed, or locked into one supplier?

12
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:54,920
Well, the trace report follows every requested entry of what was asked.

13
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:56,800
Okay. It traces it to its goal,

14
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:04,620
uh, then to the screens of that goal- Right... to the part of the application generated for it, and finally to the acceptance check written for it.

15
00:01:04,900 --> 00:01:06,400
So it tracks every single step.

16
00:01:06,740 --> 00:01:13,340
Yes. Then it lists by name every entry that is not yet traced to the end and, uh, says where each one stops.

17
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:24,060
I see. So in the fictional country of these examples, Progressa, how does the head of the ICT unit at the Ministry of Education translate this report into an actionable status for your minister?

18
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:25,120
Right. So

19
00:01:25,740 --> 00:01:29,900
the head of ICT numbers the four goals from the trace report for their application.

20
00:01:30,740 --> 00:01:34,620
Uh, number one, deciding a license- Mm-hmm... which is traced to the end.

21
00:01:35,380 --> 00:01:35,940
Number two,

22
00:01:36,580 --> 00:01:39,620
approving a change of name, which is also traced to the end.

23
00:01:39,940 --> 00:01:41,740
Okay, so those two are fully traced.

24
00:01:42,020 --> 00:01:45,640
Exactly. Now, number three, publishing the list in the Gazette.

25
00:01:46,560 --> 00:01:50,980
This has its goal and its screens, but, uh, nothing is generated for it yet.

26
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:51,820
Ah, okay.

27
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:53,160
And the fourth?

28
00:01:53,700 --> 00:01:58,900
Number four, reviewing a decision of PHEQA at a person's request.

29
00:01:59,320 --> 00:02:04,380
Right. For that one, the part is generated, but no acceptance check has been written for it.

30
00:02:04,900 --> 00:02:05,660
Okay, got it.

31
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:11,060
The head of ICT then writes the minister's status on one page in plain words

32
00:02:11,720 --> 00:02:13,980
with the date of the report beside every figure.

33
00:02:14,820 --> 00:02:21,580
So what rules ensure this data remains accurate when progress is slow or, you know, becomes costly to change?

34
00:02:22,120 --> 00:02:23,260
Well, there are three rules.

35
00:02:24,020 --> 00:02:24,520
Number one,

36
00:02:25,400 --> 00:02:28,660
a gap stays open and counted. Okay. Number two,

37
00:02:29,280 --> 00:02:32,100
a check is never changed to make its number look better.

38
00:02:32,440 --> 00:02:33,060
That makes sense.

39
00:02:33,620 --> 00:02:37,000
And number three, an empty column is information.

40
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:39,580
An empty column is information?

41
00:02:40,220 --> 00:02:40,440
Yes.

42
00:02:41,260 --> 00:02:44,700
An empty column is an honest gap that shows what is not yet done.

43
00:02:45,300 --> 00:02:47,360
It is often the most useful thing on the page.

44
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:50,360
Because it shows you exactly what's missing.

45
00:02:51,180 --> 00:02:55,720
Right. The warning sign is a supplier's report with no empty cell anywhere,

46
00:02:56,420 --> 00:02:59,100
and that is when the minister asks what was left out of it.
